      {
        "section_number": "7.7.1.1",
        "title": "Overview",
        "content_preview": "\nThe Transmission Control Protocol (TCP) allows applications of V2G Entities to establish a reliable data connection to other entities. To exchange in a reliable way and in-order delivery of sender to receiver data. Additionally TCP provides flow control and congestion control and also provides for various algorithms to handle congestion and influence flow control. \n",
        "content_length": 369,
        "level": 1,
        "classification": "general",
        "test_importance": "high",
        "requirements": [],
        "is_message_def": false,
        "message_type": null,
        "fields": [],
        "timing_params": [],
        "children": []
      },
      {
        "section_number": "7.7.1.2",
        "title": "Applicable RFCs, limitations and protocol parameter settings",
        "content_preview": "\n[V2G2-055] Each V2G Entity shall implement TCP as specified in IETF RFC 793. \n",
        "content_length": 79,
        "level": 1,
        "classification": "functional",
        "test_importance": "high",
        "requirements": [
          "V2G2-055"
        ],
        "is_message_def": false,
        "message_type": null,
        "fields": [],
        "timing_params": [],
        "children": []
      },
      {
        "section_number": "7.7.1.3",
        "title": "TCP Performance and checksum requirements",
        "content_preview": "\nThe following requirements define TCP implementation details relative to congestion control, retransmission, timing, initial window size and Selective Acknowledgement for the purpose of improving the overall performance of TCP. \n\nIt is recommended to use the following congestion control and retransmission algorithms in addition to the standard TCP methods: \n\n[V2G2-057] Each V2G Entity should implement TCP congestion control according to IETF RFC 5681. \n\n[V2G2-058] Each V2G Entity should impleme...",
        "content_length": 1281,
        "level": 1,
        "classification": "normative",
        "test_importance": "high",
        "requirements": [
          "V2G2-064",
          "V2G2-059",
          "V2G2-060",
          "V2G2-062",
          "V2G2-058",
          "V2G2-061",
          "V2G2-063",
          "V2G2-057"
        ],
        "is_message_def": false,
        "message_type": null,
        "fields": [],
        "timing_params": [],
        "children": []
      },
      {
        "section_number": "7.7.2",
        "title": "User Datagram Protocol (UDP)",
        "content_preview": "",
        "content_length": 0,
        "level": 1,
        "classification": "functional",
        "test_importance": "high",
        "requirements": [],
        "is_message_def": false,
        "message_type": null,
        "fields": [],
        "timing_params": [],
        "children": []
      },
      {
        "section_number": "7.7.2.1",
        "title": "Overview",
        "content_preview": "\nThe User Datagram Protocol (UDP) is a connectionless protocol. UDP does not provide the reliability and ordering guarantees that TCP does. Packets may arrive out of order or may be lost without notification of the sender or receiver. However, UDP is faster and more efficient for many lightweight or time-sensitive purposes. UDP is located on the Transport Layer of the OSI layered architecture model. \n\nCurrently there is no use case utilizing UDP, for which security mechanisms on UDP would be req...",
        "content_length": 565,
        "level": 1,
        "classification": "general",
        "test_importance": "high",
