      {
        "section_number": "",
        "title": "J.1 Overview",
        "content_preview": "\nIn the ISO 15118, XML signatures are used to sign e.g. sales tariffs, meter readings or complete message bodies. This chapter gives and short introduction to XML signatures. A complete example using valid data, keys and signature values is given to allow a complete understanding, how XML signatures are used for ISO 15118. \n",
        "content_length": 326,
        "level": 1,
        "classification": "general",
        "test_importance": "",
        "requirements": [],
        "is_message_def": false,
        "message_type": null,
        "fields": [],
        "timing_params": [],
        "children": []
      },
      {
        "section_number": "",
        "title": "J.2 Signature generation--``,,`",
        "content_preview": "\nSigning data using XML signatures requires two main steps: reference generation and signature generation.,``,, Reference generation includes all steps that are required to add some XML data that should be signed to the `,,`,, SignedInfo XML element. Signature generation then calculates the signature value.`,`-`-` \n\nAll EXI, hash and signature values given in this example are valid and can be checked using the following key ,`,,`- pair: - \n\n Public Key (raw x,y data): \n\n<table><tr><td>Offset(h)...",
        "content_length": 8888,
        "level": 1,
        "classification": "general",
        "test_importance": "",
        "requirements": [],
        "is_message_def": false,
        "message_type": null,
        "fields": [],
        "timing_params": [],
        "children": []
      },
      {
        "section_number": "",
        "title": "J.3 Signature generation for secondary actors",
        "content_preview": "\nFor sales tariffs the signature generation needs to be done by the secondary actor. Therefore some information about the charging session needs to be transmitted from the SECC to the SA before generating the final ChargeParameterDiscoveryRes message. This information includes necessarily the used schema version and optionally eMAID, required amount of energy, departure time, list of EVs root certificates and - if needed - some more EVSE data that can be used for sales tariff generation. The SA ...",
        "content_length": 1561,
        "level": 1,
        "classification": "general",
        "test_importance": "",
        "requirements": [
          "V2G2-307",
          "V2G2-309",
          "V2G2-308"
        ],
        "is_message_def": false,
        "message_type": null,
        "fields": [],
        "timing_params": [],
        "children": []
      },
      {
        "section_number": "",
        "title": "J.4 Signature validation",
        "content_preview": "\nValidation of XML signatures requires also two main steps, reference validation and signature validation. During reference validation first the canonicalization of the SignedInfo element is calculated. This means that the SignedInfo element is EXI encoded using the schema-informed fragment grammar based on the XMLdsig schema. \n\nThen the DigestValue element for each Reference element is checked. This requires URI de-referencing as the first step. Here the original signed data needs to be returne...",
        "content_length": 975,
        "level": 1,
        "classification": "general",
        "test_importance": "",
        "requirements": [],
        "is_message_def": false,
        "message_type": null,
        "fields": [],
